{0 Prefab loading}

Load a named entity definition from some data source, get back a live
entity with all its components attached:

{[
  module Prefab_edn = Eon_engine.Prefab_edn.Make (struct
    let path = "assets/prefabs"
  end)

  let entity = Prefab_edn.load world "goblin"
]}

[Prefab_edn] is a functor — [Prefab_edn.load] doesn't exist until it's
applied to a root directory, as above. Format-agnostic by design —
{!Eon_engine.Prefab.Make} is a functor over {i where the data comes from}
and {i how a document decomposes}, not tied to any one format.
{!Eon_engine.Prefab_edn} is the pre-built EDN instantiation shipped out of
the box; you can build your own the same way for any other format.

{1 The EDN prefab schema}

Every [.edn] prefab document is a map with up to three keys — nothing
else is special-cased:

{table
  {tr {th Key} {th Type} {th Meaning}}
  {tr {td [:extends]} {td string} {td Name of a parent prefab to inherit from}}
  {tr {td [:components]} {td map} {td Component key {i (keyword)} → component data, dispatched to a registered deserializer}}
  {tr {td [:children]} {td vector} {td Nested prefab documents — {b not} named, each spawns as its own entity {!Eon_engine.Transform_hierarchy.attach}ed to this one}}
}

All three are independently optional — [{}] is a valid document (an
entity with nothing attached). [:children]'s elements are full prefab
documents themselves, recursively the same three-key shape, so nesting
depth is unbounded.

{2 Simple example}

A single flat entity, no inheritance or nesting:

{[
  (* goblin.edn *)
  {:components
   {:Tag {:value "goblin"}
    :Velocity {:dx 0 :dy 0}}}
]}

{2 A more advanced example}

Combining all three — a base goblin, a captain variant that overrides one
field and adds a child entity, using the [#eon/percent] tag from later
in this page:

{[
  (* goblin_base.edn *)
  {:components
   {:Tag {:value "goblin"}
    :Velocity {:dx 0 :dy 0}
    :Health {:current 30 :max 30 :regen_rate #eon/percent "2%"}}}

  (* goblin_captain.edn *)
  {:extends "goblin_base"
   :components
   {:Health {:current 60 :max 60 :regen_rate #eon/percent "5%"}}
   :children
   [{:components
     {:Tag {:value "banner"}
      :Sprite {:texture_id "banner.png" :layer 1 :flip_x false :flip_y false}}}]}
]}

Loading ["goblin_captain"] produces an entity with [Health] overridden
(60/60, faster regen), [Tag]/[Velocity] inherited from [goblin_base]
untouched, and one child entity (the banner) attached via [:children] —
override, inheritance, nesting, and a tag, all in one document. See the
sections below for what each piece means and how to wire up the
[Health]/[#eon/percent] parts yourself.

{1 Using the provided EDN loader}

{2 Point it at a directory}

{!Eon_engine.Prefab_edn} is a functor of a root directory — same shape
as {!Eon_engine.Asset_lookup.Dir}'s parameter — so instantiate it once
with wherever your prefab [.edn] files live:

{[
  module Prefab_edn = Eon_engine.Prefab_edn.Make (struct
    let path = "assets/prefabs"
  end)
]}

[Prefab_edn.load world "goblin"] reads [assets/prefabs/goblin.edn].

{2 Register deserializers}

Before loading anything, every component key a prefab document might use
needs a registered {!Eon_engine.Prefab.Component_deserializer}. The
engine ships opt-in defaults for its own prefab-authorable components —
nothing is registered automatically:

{[
  let () = Eon_engine.Prefab_edn_defaults.register_all Prefab_edn.register_component
]}

This covers [Velocity], [Local_transform], [Collider], [Sprite],
[Animation], [Camera], and [Tag]. It deliberately does {b not} cover
[Parent]/[Children]/[World_transform] — those are managed by
{!Eon_engine.Transform_hierarchy}/{!Eon_engine.Transform_system}, set via
[attach]/[propagate], not authored prefab data.

{2 Write the prefab file}

{[
  {:components
   {:Tag {:value "goblin"}
    :Velocity {:dx 0 :dy 0}}}
]}

Component keys match the component's [name]
(e.g. ["Tag"], ["Velocity"], ["Local_transform"] — [PascalCase], matching
the module name), not a lowercase convention.

{2 Load it}

{[
  let () =
    let world = Eon_engine.World.create () in
    Eon_engine.Components.Engine_components.register_all world;
    let goblin = Prefab_edn.load world "goblin" in
    ignore goblin
]}

{1 Writing your own deserializer}

For a game-specific component, implement
{!Eon_engine.Prefab.Component_deserializer} and register it under the
key your prefab documents will use. This is the same shape
{!Eon_engine.Prefab_edn_defaults} uses internally for the built-in
components — small field-extraction helpers, then a record construction,
then {!Eon_engine.World.set_component} (never [add_component] — see
below):

{[
  module Health = struct
    type t = { current : int; max : int }
    let component : t Eon_engine.Components.t = Eon_engine.component "Health"
  end

  module Health_deserializer = struct
    open Eon_edn.Edn_effects

    type raw_data = value

    let field kvs name =
      match List.assoc_opt (VKeyword name) kvs with
      | Some v -> v
      | None -> failwith (Printf.sprintf "Health: missing field %S" name)

    let as_int = function VNumber n -> int_of_float n | _ -> failwith "Health: expected a number"

    let deserialize world entity ~key:_ data =
      match data with
      | VMap kvs ->
          let v = { Health.current = as_int (field kvs "current"); max = as_int (field kvs "max") } in
          Eon_engine.World.set_component world entity Health.component v
      | _ -> failwith "Health: expected a map"
  end

  let () =
    Prefab_edn.register_component "Health"
      (module Health_deserializer : Eon_engine.Prefab.Component_deserializer with type raw_data = Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value)
]}

{b Always call [set_component], never [add_component].} [set_component]
promotes to add if the component is absent, so the exact same
deserializer works both for a fresh spawn and, later, a hot-reload
re-run against an existing entity — [add_component] would raise on the
second call and force two separate code paths.

{b Don't forget to register the component type with the world too} —
this is a component lifecycle rule, not a [Prefab]-specific one:
[Prefab_edn.register_component] only wires up the {i deserializer}; the
component type itself still needs [World.register] before any entity can
carry it, the same as if you were calling [World.add_component] by hand:

{[
  let () =
    let world = Eon_engine.World.create () in
    Eon_engine.Components.Engine_components.register_all world;
    ignore (Eon_engine.World.register world Health.component)
]}

{1 Custom EDN tags in component data}

A component field can use a [#tag] to run custom logic {i during
parsing}, before the deserializer ever sees the value — the same
{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.register_tag_handler} mechanism from
[eon-edn]'s own tutorial, just used inside a prefab document instead of
a standalone EDN file.

{2 Register the tag handler}

Say prefab authors want to write a regen rate as a percentage string
(["5%"]) rather than a raw fraction ([0.05]):

{[
  let () =
    Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.register_tag_handler "eon/percent" (fun _ v ->
        match v with
        | Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VString s
          when String.length s > 0 && s.[String.length s - 1] = '%' ->
            let n = float_of_string (String.sub s 0 (String.length s - 1)) in
            Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VNumber (n /. 100.0)
        | _ -> failwith "eon/percent: expected a string like \"75%\"")
]}

Register this once, at startup, same as [Prefab_edn_defaults.register_all]
— order relative to [register_component] doesn't matter, tag handlers and
component deserializers are two independent registries.

{2 Use it in a prefab file}

{[
  {:components {:Health {:current 80 :max 100 :regen_rate #eon/percent "5%"}}}
]}

{2 The deserializer treats it like any other field}

Extending [Health] from the previous section with a [regen_rate] field —
the field extraction is ordinary, no special-casing for the fact that
this particular value happens to come from a tag:

{[
  module Health = struct
    type t = { current : int; max : int; regen_rate : float }
    let component : t Eon_engine.Components.t = Eon_engine.component "Health"
  end

  module Health_deserializer = struct
    open Eon_edn.Edn_effects

    type raw_data = value

    let field kvs name =
      match List.assoc_opt (VKeyword name) kvs with
      | Some v -> v
      | None -> failwith (Printf.sprintf "Health: missing field %S" name)

    let as_int = function VNumber n -> int_of_float n | _ -> failwith "Health: expected a number"
    let as_float = function VNumber n -> n | _ -> failwith "Health: expected a number"

    let deserialize world entity ~key:_ data =
      match data with
      | VMap kvs ->
          let v =
            { Health.current = as_int (field kvs "current")
            ; max = as_int (field kvs "max")
            ; regen_rate = as_float (field kvs "regen_rate")
            }
          in
          Eon_engine.World.set_component world entity Health.component v
      | _ -> failwith "Health: expected a map"
  end
]}

By the time [deserialize] runs, [#eon/percent "5%"] has already resolved
to [VNumber 0.05] {i during parsing} (see [eon-edn]'s tutorial for why
this happens inline, not as a second pass) — [as_float (field kvs
"regen_rate")] is indistinguishable from reading any other plain numeric
field. Verified end to end: loading a prefab with [:regen_rate
#eon/percent "5%"] produces [Health.regen_rate = 0.05].

{1 Inheritance and nested entities}

{2 [:extends]}

A prefab can extend another by name; the base's components load first,
the extending prefab's data overrides/adds on top field-by-field:

{[
  (* goblin_base.edn *)
  {:components {:Tag {:value "goblin"} :Velocity {:dx 0 :dy 0}}}

  (* goblin_archer.edn *)
  {:extends "goblin_base"
   :components {:Velocity {:dx 5 :dy 0}}}
]}

Loading ["goblin_archer"] produces an entity with [Velocity.dx = 5]
(overridden) and [Tag.value = "goblin"] (inherited, untouched). Chains
resolve to arbitrary depth (["archer"] extends ["goblin"] extends
["humanoid_base"] costs nothing extra), and a cyclic chain raises
[Failure "prefab inheritance cycle: ..."] rather than hanging.

{2 [:children]}

A prefab can declare nested entities; each spawns as its own entity,
wired to the parent via {!Eon_engine.Transform_hierarchy.attach} — the
same primitive used for manual world setup, not special prefab-only
hierarchy machinery:

{[
  {:components {:Tag {:value "turret"}}
   :children
   [{:components {:Tag {:value "turret_base"}}}
    {:components {:Tag {:value "turret_barrel"}}}]}
]}

The root's {!Eon_engine.Components.Children} component ends up with both
child entities; each child's {!Eon_engine.Components.Parent} points back
at the root.

{1 Building a custom instantiation}

EDN isn't the only option. {!Eon_engine.Prefab.Make} takes two arguments:

{table
  {tr {th Module type} {th Responsibility}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Prefab.Source}} {td Where the data comes from — [load : string -> raw_data]}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Prefab.Document_shape}} {td How to decompose a document — [extends_of]/[components_of]/[children_of]/[merge]}}
}

A minimal source with no real inheritance support — everything else
(the registry, [load]'s tail-recursive spawn work-list, cycle detection)
comes for free from {!Eon_engine.Prefab.Make}:

{[
  module My_source = struct
    type raw_data = My_format.t
    let load name = My_format.read_file (name ^ ".myfmt")
  end

  module My_document = struct
    type raw_data = My_format.t
    let extends_of _ = None                       (* no inheritance support *)
    let components_of doc = My_format.components doc
    let children_of _doc = []                      (* no nesting support *)
    let merge _base override = override             (* degenerate: override always wins *)
  end

  module My_prefab = Eon_engine.Prefab.Make (My_source) (My_document)
]}

[My_prefab.register_component]/[My_prefab.load] work exactly like
[Prefab_edn]'s — same API, independent of [eon_edn] entirely.

{1 Asset_lookup}

{!Eon_engine.Asset_lookup} maps logical string ids to absolute file paths —
the same directory-is-the-manifest idea {!Eon_engine.Prefab_edn} uses for
prefab files, generalised to any asset kind. Backends receive a module
satisfying {!Eon_engine.Asset_lookup.S} at [init] time and walk it once
(via [iter]) to pre-load assets into their own internal handles; game code
never sees those handles, only the logical id strings, forever:

{[
  assets/
    sounds/hit.wav   →  logical_id: "sounds/hit.wav"
    music/theme.ogg  →  logical_id: "music/theme.ogg"
]}

{table
  {tr {th Module} {th Behaviour}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Asset_lookup.Dir}} {td Scans a root directory recursively at every [iter] call — hot-reloading friendly, no code changes needed to pick up a new file}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Asset_lookup.Null}} {td Always empty — CI and tests that need no real files}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Asset_lookup.Scripted}} {td Hand-maintained [(logical_id, absolute_path)] list — tests that need precise control over which assets are visible}}
}

{[
  open Eon_engine

  module Assets = Asset_lookup.Dir (struct
    let path = "assets/prefabs"
  end)

  let () =
    Assets.iter (fun logical_id absolute_path ->
      Printf.printf "%s -> %s\n" logical_id absolute_path)

  let () =
    Asset_lookup.Scripted.set [ ("sounds/hit.wav", "/tmp/hit.wav") ];
    Asset_lookup.Scripted.iter (fun id path ->
      Printf.printf "%s -> %s\n" id path)
]}

There is no central asset manager anywhere in the engine — each backend
(rendering, audio) owns its own GPU/audio handles from [init] to
[shutdown], resolved from the same logical ids an [Asset_lookup.S] walked
at startup. See {{!page-rendering}Rendering}'s "Asset loading" section for
the backend side of this contract.

